WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.014 --> 00:00:14.015
MNT REFORM 2 · HOW TO APPROACH
A real laptop mainboard that will not start
An open-hardware laptop board: 529 parts, a 30 V input stage, and converters making +5V, +3V3 and +1V2.
Symptom: it draws power but the system never starts.
You will learn: reading a power sequence, measuring rails at capacitors, and finding a shorted rail with the thermal camera.

2
00:00:14.713 --> 00:00:32.917
STEP 1 · POWER-UP
Watch the bench supply while the board powers up: it steps through power states.
Why: A mainboard turns its rails on in order. If a rail fails, the controller stops or retries, and the supply current shows it.

3
00:00:32.928 --> 00:00:47.399
STEP 2 · MEASURE THE RAILS
Measure each rail at a decoupling capacitor. +5V at C35: +4.997 V. Fine.
Why: Every rail has capacitors right next to the parts it feeds. Their pads are the easiest place to probe a rail.

4
00:00:47.412 --> 00:00:59.414
STEP 2 · MEASURE THE RAILS
+3V3 at C29: +0.640 V, far below 3.3 V.
Why: A converter that is running but cannot hold its voltage is being dragged down: something on the rail draws more current than it can deliver.

5
00:00:59.415 --> 00:01:11.947
STEP 3 · FIND THE SHORT
A rail pulled low by a short turns that current into heat at the shorted part.
Why: Power = I² × R. The fault carries amps, so it warms up faster than anything else on the rail.

6
00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:18.452
STEP 3 · FIND THE SHORT
Switch the view to Thermal and give it a few seconds: look for the spot that heats first.

7
00:01:18.462 --> 00:01:32.463
WHAT YOU LEARNED
Rails first, then heat
Measure every rail at its capacitors: a low rail with a running converter means overload.
A short is a heater: use the thermal view, freeze spray, IPA or rosin (R) to see which part warms first.
Many parts share one rail: the heat points to the one that matters.
